## Why This Is Asked

This question explores advanced incremental data-structure design under streaming constraints, requiring mastery of amortized analysis for batched updates alongside exact substring query capabilities.

## Key Concepts

- **Suffix Automaton fundamentals**: States, transitions, links, and the endpos equivalence relation
- **Batched extension strategies**: Buffering B characters before committing to the global structure
- **Clone management and amortized cost**: Deferred clone creation and lazy propagation techniques
- **Transition representations**: Compact maps vs. dense arrays for cache locality
- **Trade-off analysis**: Balancing batch size B, alphabet size σ, and total string length N

## Code Example

```cpp
struct State {
    int link;
    int len;
    std::unordered_map<char, int> next;  // or vector<int> for small σ
};

class BatchedSuffixAutomaton {
    std::vector<State> st;
    int last;
    std::string buffer;
    const size_t B;

public:
    BatchedSuffixAutomaton(size_t batch_size = 1024) : B(batch_size), last(0) {
        st.push_back({-1, 0, {}});
    }

    void extend(char c) {
        buffer.push_back(c);
        if (buffer.size() < B) return;
        flushBuffer();
    }

    void flushBuffer() {
        for (char c : buffer) {
            int cur = st.size();
            st.push_back({0, st[last].len + 1, {}});
            int p = last;
            while (p >= 0 && !st[p].next.count(c)) {
                st[p].next[c] = cur;
                p = st[p].link;
            }
            if (p == -1) {
                st[cur].link = 0;
            } else {
                int q = st[p].next[c];
                if (st[p].len + 1 == st[q].len) {
                    st[cur].link = q;
                } else {
                    int clone = st.size();
                    st.push_back(st[q]);  // clone
                    st[clone].len = st[p].len + 1;
                    while (p >= 0 && st[p].next[c] == q) {
                        st[p].next[c] = clone;
                        p = st[p].link;
                    }
                    st[q].link = st[cur].link = clone;
                }
            }
            last = cur;
        }
        buffer.clear();
    }

    bool query(const std::string& p) {
        int v = 0;
        for (char c : p) {
            if (!st[v].next.count(c)) return false;
            v = st[v].next[c];
        }
        return true;
    }
};
```

## Optimal Batch Size

The optimal B depends on workload characteristics:
- **Small σ (e.g., DNA, σ ≤ 4)**: Larger B (4096+) works well; dense transition arrays gain from cache locality
- **Large σ (e.g., Unicode, σ ≈ 10⁵)**: Smaller B (128–512); sparse maps reduce memory pressure
- **Memory-constrained environments**: B ≈ √N balances update frequency against per-batch overhead
- **Latency-sensitive queries**: Smaller B reduces worst-case query delay between flushes

## Why This Is Asked

This problem tests real-time data tracking with batched updates and sliding window counts. It evaluates the ability to design a data structure that combines efficient timestamp-based aggregation with per-session lookup while managing amortized cleanup.

## Key Concepts

- Batched online updates with timestamp ordering
- Sliding window counts with expiration
- Two-tier data structures (ordered map + hash map)
- Amortized analysis versus naive recalculation

## Code Example

```javascript
class SessionTracker {
  constructor(W) {
    this.W = W;
    this.idToTime = new Map();
    this.timeToCount = new Map();
  }

  processBatch(events, currentTime) {
    for (const { sessionId, time, isNew } of events) {
      if (isNew) {
        this.idToTime.set(sessionId, time);
        this.timeToCount.set(time, (this.timeToCount.get(time) || 0) + 1);
      } else {
        const oldTime = this.idToTime.get(sessionId);
        this.timeToCount.set(oldTime, this.timeToCount.get(oldTime) - 1);
        this.idToTime.set(sessionId, time);
        this.timeToCount.set(time, (this.timeToCount.get(time) || 0) + 1);
      }
    }
    this.expireOldSessions(currentTime);
  }

  expireOldSessions(currentTime) {
    const threshold = currentTime - this.W;
    for (const [time, count] of this.timeToCount) {
      if (time < threshold) {
        this.timeToCount.delete(time);
      }
    }
  }

  countActive(currentTime) {
    const threshold = currentTime - this.W;
    let count = 0;
    for (const [time, c] of this.timeToCount) {
      if (time >= threshold) count += c;
    }
    return count;
  }
}
```

## Analysis

**Preprocessing**: O(1) — just initialize empty maps.

**Memory**: O(S) where S is the number of unique timestamps with active sessions; worst case O(N) for N sessions if all have distinct timestamps.

**Update**: Each event is O(log T) for the ordered map insertion/deletion, where T is the number of unique timestamps. With B events per batch, this is O(B log T) amortized.

**Query**: O(T) in the worst case — must scan all timestamps in the window. Using an ordered map with prefix sum augmentation could reduce this to O(log T).

**Comparison to naive recalculation**: Recalculating from scratch after each batch would require O(N) per query, where N is total active sessions. The two-tier structure provides O(1) lookup per session update and O(T) or O(log T) queries — a significant improvement when batches are processed frequently and queries are intermittent.

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests understanding of sliding-window quantile estimation with bounded memory—a practical problem in latency monitoring where old samples must fade out over time.

## Key Concepts
- Sliding window maintenance using a circular buffer
- Fixed-bin histogram design and bucket mapping
- Error bounds derived from bucket width

## Bucket Mapping
The value range [0, M) is divided into S equal-width buckets. A value x maps to bucket floor(x * S / M), giving each bucket a width of M/S.

## Choosing S
The number of buckets S trades off memory (O(S)) and accuracy. With bucket width M/S, the P95 estimate has error at most M/(2S). For 1% error, choose S ≈ 50*M. In practice, S = 100-1000 works well for typical latency ranges.

## Error Guarantees vs Exact Computation
- **Approximate P95**: The estimated percentile falls within the bucket containing the true P95. Maximum error is half the bucket width: M/(2S).
- **Exact P95**: Requires maintaining all W samples in the window and sorting them, which is O(W log W) per query.
- **Trade-off**: This histogram approach gives O(1) updates and O(S) queries with bounded error, vs O(1) updates and O(W log W) queries for exact computation.

## Code Example
```javascript
class SlidingWindowP95 {
  constructor(M, W, S) {
    this.M = M;
    this.W = W;
    this.S = S;
    this.buffer = new Int32Array(W);
    this.hist = new Int32Array(S);
    this.head = 0;
    this.count = 0;
  }

  add(x) {
    const b = Math.floor((x * this.S) / this.M);
    this.hist[b]++;

    if (this.count === this.W) {
      const y = this.buffer[this.head];
      const by = Math.floor((y * this.S) / this.M);
      this.hist[by]--;
    } else {
      this.count++;
    }

    this.buffer[this.head] = x;
    this.head = (this.head + 1) % this.W;
  }

  query() {
    const p95 = Math.floor(0.95 * this.count);
    let cum = 0;
    for (let b = 0; b < this.S; b++) {
      cum += this.hist[b];
      if (cum >= p95) return (b * this.M) / this.S;
    }
    return this.M;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for non-uniform distributions or dynamic M?
- How does the choice of S affect query latency in a high-throughput system?

## Why This Is Asked
Understanding how to count inversions online with limited memory and batched updates tests both data structures and amortized reasoning.

## Key Concepts
- Fenwick tree for prefix sums; online per-element updates
- Inversion counting formula: add current_seen - count_of_≤v
- Amortized batch analysis and memory scaling with M

## Code Example
```javascript
class Fenwick {
  constructor(n){ this.f=[0]; for(let i=1;i<=n;i++) this.f[i]=0; }
  add(i,delta){ for(i+=1;i<this.f.length;i+=i&-i) this.f[i]+=delta }
  sum(i){ let s=0; for(i+=1;i>0;i-=i&-i) s+=this.f[i]; return s }
}
function processBatch(batch, M, fen, totalSeen){ let inversions=0; for(const v of batch){ inversions += totalSeen - fen.sum(v); fen.add(v,1); totalSeen++; } return {inversions, totalSeen}; }
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle 64-bit inversion counts on large streams?
- Compare to using a segment tree; when is it advantageous?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to design incremental single-source shortest paths with batch updates and to reason about amortized work and practical data structures.

## Key Concepts
- Incremental SSSP with batch edge insertions
- Localized relaxation and pruning
- Priority queues and potential bucketed variants (Dial's)
- Amortized analysis and space efficiency

## Code Example
```javascript
function updateBatch(batchEdges){
  for (const [u,v,w] of batchEdges){
    // ensure undirected view if needed
    adj[u].push([v,w]);
    adj[v].push([u,w]);
  }
  for (const [u,v,w] of batchEdges){
    if (dist[v] > dist[u] + w){
      dist[v] = dist[u] + w;
      pq.push([dist[v], v]);
    }
    if (dist[u] > dist[v] + w){
      dist[u] = dist[v] + w;
      pq.push([dist[u], u]);
    }
  }
  while (!pq.isEmpty()){
    const [d, x] = pq.pop();
    if (d !== dist[x]) continue;
    for (const [y,w] of adj[x]){
      if (dist[y] > dist[x] + w){
        dist[y] = dist[x] + w;
        pq.push([dist[y], y]);
      }
    }
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical thinking on append-heavy data structures, cache-aware trade-offs, and I/O modeling under realistic constraints.

## Key Concepts
- Two-tier indexing
- Amortized cost analysis
- Cache & I/O modeling
- Rebuild strategies and hot/cold separation

## Code Example
```javascript
class TieredIndex {
  constructor(B) { this.B=B; this.t1=new Map(); this.t2=new Map(); }
  append(blockId, offset) { this.t1.set(blockId, offset); /* trigger rebuild when needed */ }
  lookup(blockId) { if (this.t1.has(blockId)) return this.t1.get(blockId); return this.t2.get(blockId); }
  rebuild() { for (let [k,v] of this.t1) this.t2.set(k,v); this.t1.clear(); }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would this adapt if blocks could be deleted or updated? 
- How would you empirically validate the cost model with real workloads?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of streaming distinct-count with memory constraints and practical trade-offs between accuracy and memory.

## Key Concepts
- HyperLogLog sketch
- register count m controls error
- per-element O(1) update, O(m) memory, mergeable sketches

## Code Example
```javascript
class HyperLogLog {
  constructor(m = 16) {
    this.m = m; this.registers = new Uint8Array(m);
  }
  hash(x) {
    let h = 2166136261;
    for (let i = 0; i < x.length; i++) h = (h ^ x.charCodeAt(i)) * 16777619 >>> 0;
    return h;
  }
  add(x) {
    const h = this.hash(x);
    const idx = h & (this.m - 1);
    const w = h >>> Math.log2(this.m);
    const leading = Math.clz32(w) + 1;
    this.registers[idx] = Math.max(this.registers[idx], leading);
  }
  estimate() {
    let sum = 0;
    for (const r of this.registers) sum += Math.pow(2, -r);
    const alpha = 0.7213 / (1 + 1.079 / this.m);
    return alpha * this.m * this.m / sum;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How does choosing m affect relative error and memory? 
- How would you merge two sketches in a distributed setup? 
- What pitfalls exist with non-uniform ID distributions?

